## Findings

[R4-F1] **MEDIUM** — the parity ratchet’s excluded corner is reachable, and it changes a valid CLI exit.

The test labels `key_field="custom", payload={"custom": 0}` unreachable ([test_key_predicate_parity.py](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/apps/loops/tests/test_key_predicate_parity.py:20)), but the declared `by` fold accepts every non-`None` key, including numeric zero ([engine.py](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/atoms/src/atoms/engine.py:133)).

Empirical reproduction with a real declared fold and stored JSON numeric key:

```text
_item_matches_key(..., "custom", "0") = True
_row_matches_key(..., "0")           = False
surface comma-OR survivors            = 0

read ... --key 0,missing
exit 0, "No data yet."

read ... --key 0,missing --status open
exit 2, statusless-kind refusal
```

`_row_key` nulls the falsy key ([surface.py](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/apps/loops/src/loops/surface.py:507)), while the census predicate still scans the custom field ([fetch.py](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/apps/loops/src/loops/commands/fetch.py:368)). Thus the census is not operating on the post-Surface row set as claimed ([dispatch.py](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/apps/loops/src/loops/cli/dispatch.py:148)). The 15×20 matrix otherwise covers the per-token comma-OR predicate shape adequately—`any()` composition follows from cell parity—but deliberately omits the known reachable disagreement.

